    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a daily create-limit rule to `POST /api/owners`: once **100 or more owners already carry today's `registrationDate`**, further creation is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests**.\n\nFollowing the existing `EnsureCityCapacity` pattern, I added four files and wired one step:\n\n1. **`EnsureDailyCreateLimit.java`** \u2014 new pipeline step. Counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` via `ownerRepository.findAll()`; throws `DailyCreateLimitException` when the count is `>= 100`. Runs after `ValidateOwnerFields` and before `BuildOwner`, so the owner being created isn't yet counted (matching how `BuildOwner` defaults `registrationDate` to today for new owners).\n\n2. **`DailyCreateLimitException.java`** \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause and routes to escalation.\n\n3. **`DailyCreateLimitExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `429` with body `{\"errors\":[\"registrationDate\"]}`, mirroring the shape used by the other owner handlers.\n\n4. **`...DailyCreateLimitException.yml`** escalation mapping.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureDailyCreateLimit` step (governed by `transaction`) between `ensureCityCapacity` and `build`.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (creates 100 owners, expects the 101st to return 429) passes, and the full suite reports `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne note on interpretation: the spec says \"created today (by registrationDate),\" so the check counts existing owners dated today against `LocalDate.now()`, independent of any `registrationDate` supplied in the request body. That matches the test, which creates owners with no explicit date (defaulted to today).",
